/**
 * DIN-276 regulation-table seed builders (Plan 3 Task 13, 2026-09-18).
 *
 * SR-1: every seeded cell and every `verbatim_quote` was read in this session from
 * the transcript `Desktop\Guidelines\DWA DIN Scribd\DIN-276\DIN-276.md` (English
 * translation of DIN 276:2018-12; 1548 lines, read end-to-end). The row spans were
 * lifted MECHANICALLY by line range (a throwaway generator in the scratchpad walked
 * the `\hline NNN & …` rows, tracking the nested `\begin{tabular}{l}` depth so a
 * multi-line notes cell ends the row at depth 0; every string went through
 * `JSON.stringify`, so no `${ }` / backtick hazards). The transcript line of every
 * row is in `line` (a multi-line row also carries `endLine`; a `\multirow` block
 * row carries `spanFrom`/`spanTo` — the block rows share the block span). Every
 * seeded cell is asserted inside its span at build time (`inSpan`; a multi-line
 * notes cell fragment by fragment). Verified by
 * `scripts/regulation-tables/verify-regulation-tables.ts din276 "<transcript>"`.
 * Emitted as `20260917101300_regulation_tables_seed_din276.sql` (no earlier
 * DIN-276 table seed exists — nothing superseded).
 *
 * Edition: the transcript's running caption prints "DIN 276:2018-12" (L516 and
 * every following table page); L1 "Replacement for DIN 277-3:2005-04,
 * DIN 276-1:2008-12 and DIN 276-4:2009-08" = prod `standards.version`
 * 'December 2018 (DIN 276:2018-12)' (read-only 2026-09-18) → `'2018-12'`.
 *
 * Tables:
 *   TABLE1 (L464–L1010) — Table 1 "Cost breakdown": one row per printed KG
 *     (code, designation, notes) + the STRUCTURAL columns `level` (1/2/3 from the
 *     three-digit ordinal, §5.1 L424 "three levels, which are characterised by
 *     three-digit ordinal numbers"), `parent_kg`, `kg1` (first-level ancestor) and `kg2`
 *     (second-level ancestor, null on a first-level row) — not printed per row (din276-J-2);
 *     `kg1_key` = the TABLE2 key of the first-level ancestor (`kg_N00`, a lookup argument only:
 *     L1022 "Unless further specifications are made under 6.3 and Table 3 as well as under 6.4
 *     and Table 4, this also applies to the respective cost groups of the second and third
 *     level" — the register's unit chain falls back to Table 2 through it). Policy `anhaltswert` for the notes (L457 "… are
 *     examples of the respective cost group. The list is not exhaustive."), the
 *     structure itself is binding (§4.2.2 L215) — both fragments in the composed
 *     `override_quote`. `md_verified` (every printed row lifted; blank notes cells
 *     are printed blank, not unreadable).
 *   TABLE2 (L1030–L1037) — Table 2 first-level quantities / reference units;
 *     `anhaltswert` (L1022 "It is recommended that …"); `md_verified`.
 *   TABLE3 (L1052–L1160) — Table 3 KG 300 units; `anhaltswert` (L1044); the row
 *     340 prints an EMPTY unit cell (L1093; every sibling prints m²) — kept null,
 *     din276-U-1 → `imported_unverified`.
 *   TABLE4 (L1174–L1541) — Table 4 KG 400 extended breakdown keyed (kg, nr): nr
 *     "0" = the printed KG row, "1"… = the printed numbered items; `kann` (L1166
 *     "… the extended breakdown in Table 4 … can be used"; din276-O-1 proposes
 *     `anhaltswert` from §6.1 L1018 "It is therefore recommended …"). Printed-blank
 *     cells that are physically ambiguous (440 head row: designation printed, unit
 *     blank, L1287; 458 item 6 "Transport telematics": unit blank, L1375; 474 item
 *     3 unit "m." L1450) are kept as printed — din276-U-2 → `imported_unverified`.
 *
 * Unit cells: the transcript prints `$\mathrm{m}^{2}$` / `$\mathrm{m}^{3}$` — seeded
 * as "m²" / "m³" with the printed TeX token asserted in the span (`unit_tex`);
 * every other unit ("m", "St.", "kW", "m3/h", "kVA", "Ah", "kWp", "kvar", "m.") is
 * kept verbatim.
 *
 * String-literal trap (probed 2026-09-18): in the expression language a quoted RHS of
 * `==` / `!=` that names a VALUED symbol in scope (a register column or a worksheet
 * field) resolves to that symbol's value (`compare` node, the legacy var-vs-var rule) —
 * `status == 'auftrag'` with a column `auftrag` compares against the amount, never the
 * string. Every enum token / structural value compared by literal in a row expression
 * is therefore chosen so it is NOT an identifier in scope: `kg1` / `kg2` are printed as
 * "KG 300" / "KG 310" (a space is never part of an identifier); amount columns beside a
 * status enum are suffixed `_eur`.
 *
 * Key tokens (G-A3): TABLE1 / TABLE2 / TABLE3 `kg` = `kg_<code>` — the prod field
 * symbols of DIN-276-09 … -16 (`kg_311` …), also the options of the CREATED
 * `kg_selector` (TABLE2 keys) and of the register `kg` lookup_key columns; TABLE4
 * `nr` = the printed item number as a string.
 */
import type { RegulationTable, RegulationRow, ValueColumn } from './regulation-tables';

